\title{Choice Data}
\description{
An object of the \code{choice_data} R6 class stores the data for choice modeling
and can interpret \code{data.frame}s in wide or long format.

\strong{Wide data} contain one row per choice occasion. Alternative-varying
covariates are supplied in columns with names of the form
\code{covariate_alternative}, for example \code{price_bus} and \code{price_car};
alternative-constant covariates are supplied as ordinary columns. The
argument \code{column_choice} names the column that contains the chosen
alternative label. The argument \code{column_decider_id} optionally names the
decider identifier column. If it is \code{NULL}, each row is treated as one
decider with one choice occasion. The argument \code{column_occasion_id}
optionally names a choice occasion identifier column. The argument
\code{column_alternative} is not used for wide data.

\strong{Long data} contain one row per alternative and choice occasion.
Alternative-varying covariates are supplied as ordinary columns, one value
per alternative row; alternative-constant covariates are supplied as columns
that are constant within each choice set. The argument \code{column_alternative}
is required and names the column that contains the alternative labels. The
argument \code{column_choice} names the column that identifies the chosen row in
each choice set, encoded by a logical indicator or a \code{0}/\code{1} numeric
indicator. Choice sets are identified by \code{column_decider_id} and
\code{column_occasion_id} when both are supplied, by consecutive complete
alternative blocks within each decider when only \code{column_decider_id} is
supplied, and by consecutive complete alternative blocks when neither is
supplied.

For a given \code{\link{mod}} object,
\itemize{
\item choice data can be simulated via the method \verb{$simulate()},
\item choices can be redrawn via the method \verb{$redraw_choices()},
\item design matrices are created via the method \verb{$build_design()}.
}
}
